1
00:00:48,006 --> 00:00:54,971
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

2
00:00:56,973 --> 00:00:57,807
Madam.

3
00:01:02,896 --> 00:01:05,315
I think there's a way…

4
00:01:07,484 --> 00:01:08,735
to get rid of Sato.

5
00:01:10,195 --> 00:01:11,446
What do you mean?

6
00:01:14,282 --> 00:01:15,158
I'm home.

7
00:01:28,296 --> 00:01:29,422
You're both here.

8
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:31,925
You're home.

9
00:01:33,176 --> 00:01:34,010
This works out then.

10
00:01:52,529 --> 00:01:53,404
Nice to meet you.

11
00:01:53,488 --> 00:01:55,198
Let me introduce myself properly.

12
00:01:56,324 --> 00:01:57,325
I'm with the Gugukdan.

13
00:01:58,243 --> 00:01:59,160
I'm Hong Seog-won.

14
00:02:26,855 --> 00:02:27,814
EPISODE 14

15
00:02:27,897 --> 00:02:30,692
We're basically trapped in this house,

16
00:02:30,775 --> 00:02:32,944
and Sato is threatening you.

17
00:02:33,027 --> 00:02:35,989
What exactly is your plan
in a situation like this?

18
00:02:36,072 --> 00:02:39,075
Sato, Fukuda, and Kim Seung-eok.

19
00:02:39,159 --> 00:02:41,244
I think these three will go
for each other's throats.

20
00:02:43,955 --> 00:02:45,915
So those three will turn on each other?

21
00:02:45,999 --> 00:02:46,833
Yes.

22
00:02:47,584 --> 00:02:49,169
Sato's already backed into a corner.

23
00:02:49,252 --> 00:02:50,795
With one incident after another,

24
00:02:50,879 --> 00:02:52,505
the pressure from above must be immense.

25
00:02:52,589 --> 00:02:53,548
That's right.

26
00:02:54,299 --> 00:02:55,383
Sato and Fukuda,

27
00:02:56,551 --> 00:02:59,053
it'll be hard for both
to keep their posts.

28
00:02:59,137 --> 00:03:02,265
At least one of them
is bound to be held responsible.

29
00:03:03,057 --> 00:03:05,852
They'll both want
to shift the blame onto each other.

30
00:03:05,935 --> 00:03:07,020
That's precisely it.

31
00:03:07,604 --> 00:03:10,940
I suppose that's why
Fukuda threatened me today.

32
00:03:11,024 --> 00:03:13,526
Find dirt on the inspector general.

33
00:03:13,610 --> 00:03:15,069
And whatever you see or hear,

34
00:03:15,153 --> 00:03:17,780
you report to me first,
before the inspector general.

35
00:03:19,574 --> 00:03:20,617
Fukuda

36
00:03:21,367 --> 00:03:23,578
is planning to turn on Sato?

37
00:03:25,121 --> 00:03:26,664
It'd be quite a coup,

38
00:03:26,748 --> 00:03:29,042
but Fukuda won't make
a move without leverage.

39
00:03:30,001 --> 00:03:32,295
Do we have anything
Fukuda could use against Sato?

40
00:03:33,046 --> 00:03:35,131
We didn't, but now we do.

41
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:39,928
Like I said, there are three of them,
including Kim Seung-eok.

42
00:03:40,011 --> 00:03:44,265
It seems Kim Seung-eok is itching
to call off the engagement with Sato.

43
00:03:44,349 --> 00:03:47,518
Gyodang-ri is hardly
the issue right now, isn't it?

44
00:03:47,602 --> 00:03:48,978
I want to call off the engagement.

45
00:03:52,148 --> 00:03:55,109
So, here's what I have in mind.

46
00:03:56,986 --> 00:03:57,820
What?

47
00:03:58,780 --> 00:04:00,907
You want to do what with me?

48
00:04:05,119 --> 00:04:06,287
Let's make a deal.

49
00:04:08,831 --> 00:04:11,584
You called me out here this late,
only to spring this on me?

50
00:04:11,668 --> 00:04:14,420
There's a shantytown called Gyodang-ri.

51
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:18,049
Your father recently bought the land
from the Government-General.

52
00:04:20,218 --> 00:04:21,928
I'm not interested
in my father's business.

53
00:04:22,929 --> 00:04:24,347
You will be now.

54
00:04:24,430 --> 00:04:25,765
Your father and mine

55
00:04:26,391 --> 00:04:28,726
are trying to drive out the Joseon people

56
00:04:30,311 --> 00:04:32,480
who live there
and have nowhere else to go.

57
00:04:35,650 --> 00:04:38,069
Why are you telling me this?

58
00:04:40,238 --> 00:04:43,116
Go talk to Ms. Myung-nam. She'll help us.

59
00:04:43,199 --> 00:04:45,660
Don't ask me anything further.

60
00:04:47,954 --> 00:04:49,080
I need to borrow some money.

61
00:04:51,666 --> 00:04:52,500
I need you

62
00:04:53,209 --> 00:04:54,460
to lend me 500,000 won.

63
00:04:54,544 --> 00:04:55,461
Sorry?

64
00:04:57,088 --> 00:04:58,131
Five hundred thousand won?

65
00:04:58,214 --> 00:05:00,925
I intend to buy the land from your father.

66
00:05:01,467 --> 00:05:02,927
I want those people

67
00:05:04,387 --> 00:05:05,680
to live there in peace.

68
00:05:06,889 --> 00:05:08,641
But I can't afford it on my own.

69
00:05:08,725 --> 00:05:11,227
So I really need your help.

70
00:05:11,311 --> 00:05:12,437
If you lend me 500,000 won,

71
00:05:13,062 --> 00:05:15,815
your father's money, to be precise,

72
00:05:17,150 --> 00:05:20,111
then I'll buy the land and give it to you.

73
00:05:21,696 --> 00:05:24,240
You'll buy the land and give it to me?

74
00:05:25,408 --> 00:05:26,242
Earlier today,

75
00:05:26,951 --> 00:05:29,162
I overheard your father and mine arguing.

76
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:31,456
Apparently,

77
00:05:31,539 --> 00:05:33,791
our marriage arrangement involved

78
00:05:35,209 --> 00:05:36,627
a financial transaction.

79
00:05:38,212 --> 00:05:39,088
What do you mean?

80
00:05:39,672 --> 00:05:43,426
The Government-General owned
the Gyodang-ri site, worth 700,000 won,

81
00:05:44,052 --> 00:05:46,220
but my father sold it
to Mr. Kim for 400,000 won

82
00:05:46,304 --> 00:05:48,056
in exchange for rapid development.

83
00:05:48,139 --> 00:05:49,390
MARRIAGE CONTRACT

84
00:05:49,474 --> 00:05:51,768
A 300,000-WON DOWRY
TO BE PAID WITHIN 5 DAYS

85
00:05:51,851 --> 00:05:53,853
<i>I doubt that alone</i>
<i>got him a 300,000-won discount.</i>

86
00:06:01,152 --> 00:06:03,279
<i>One party gets political funding,</i>

87
00:06:04,113 --> 00:06:06,366
<i>while the other profits</i>
<i>from future business deals.</i>

88
00:06:09,535 --> 00:06:10,828
Dad…

89
00:06:10,912 --> 00:06:14,791
It seems they put the whole arrangement
in the marriage contract.

90
00:06:14,874 --> 00:06:17,919
It's called a marriage contract,
but in reality,

91
00:06:18,002 --> 00:06:20,963
it's evidence that the inspector general
sold Government-General property

92
00:06:21,047 --> 00:06:22,715
below value in exchange for a kickback.

93
00:06:23,299 --> 00:06:24,634
Let's find it somehow

94
00:06:26,094 --> 00:06:27,595
and put an end to this marriage.

95
00:06:30,390 --> 00:06:32,183
I assume you don't have
any feelings for me.

96
00:06:32,266 --> 00:06:34,227
Of course not. Not one bit.

97
00:06:36,312 --> 00:06:37,146
I see.

98
00:06:38,064 --> 00:06:40,691
Even so, how am I supposed to trust you?

99
00:06:40,775 --> 00:06:42,860
It's hard to believe you're doing this

100
00:06:42,944 --> 00:06:44,487
out of concern for the Joseon people.

101
00:06:47,824 --> 00:06:49,200
I'm not like my father.

102
00:06:50,743 --> 00:06:51,994
Just as you're not like

103
00:06:53,121 --> 00:06:54,372
your father.

104
00:06:59,710 --> 00:07:06,134
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

105
00:07:25,445 --> 00:07:26,863
Good morning, sir.

106
00:07:34,579 --> 00:07:35,746
- Good morning.
- Good morning.

107
00:07:38,124 --> 00:07:38,958
Follow me.

108
00:07:59,479 --> 00:08:01,606
Father must be keeping
those important documents

109
00:08:02,523 --> 00:08:04,233
in his office cabinet.

110
00:08:04,984 --> 00:08:07,904
<i>There should be a safe</i>
<i>in the cabinet behind his desk.</i>

111
00:08:08,946 --> 00:08:09,780
Did you bring me

112
00:08:11,032 --> 00:08:12,116
the evidence?

113
00:08:17,747 --> 00:08:18,998
Did you even try looking?

114
00:08:19,749 --> 00:08:22,418
Surely you don't think
I'm going to forget about this.

115
00:08:23,669 --> 00:08:24,629
No, I don't, sir.

116
00:08:29,050 --> 00:08:30,468
I gave you five days.

117
00:08:30,551 --> 00:08:31,552
Now you have three.

118
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:34,180
Prove they're with the Gugukdan.

119
00:08:34,263 --> 00:08:35,890
Whether it's the woman or Yoo Philip,

120
00:08:36,641 --> 00:08:37,892
either will do.

121
00:08:40,978 --> 00:08:42,396
Three days isn't enough.

122
00:08:44,398 --> 00:08:47,235
They did pull some strings
to get me into the Government-General,

123
00:08:47,318 --> 00:08:48,736
but like I said,

124
00:08:48,819 --> 00:08:51,405
I really had no idea
they were involved with the Gugukdan.

125
00:08:51,489 --> 00:08:52,323
So please,

126
00:08:52,865 --> 00:08:54,784
give me some time to find proof--

127
00:08:54,867 --> 00:08:56,327
Keum Seo-hee.

128
00:08:59,205 --> 00:09:01,374
If you bring me nothing in three days,

129
00:09:01,457 --> 00:09:05,169
I'll round them all up,
even if they're American.

130
00:09:05,253 --> 00:09:06,712
On top of that,

131
00:09:06,796 --> 00:09:08,297
you'll be in trouble too.

132
00:09:09,423 --> 00:09:10,716
I'm sure you know I was

133
00:09:11,342 --> 00:09:13,678
a skilled higher police officer
at Jongno Police Station.

134
00:09:18,432 --> 00:09:19,475
You've got three days.

135
00:09:20,226 --> 00:09:21,269
Not a day longer.

136
00:09:27,650 --> 00:09:29,026
Damn it.

137
00:09:32,071 --> 00:09:34,240
You can't even handle one simple job.

138
00:09:34,323 --> 00:09:35,950
How long will you drag this out?

139
00:09:36,033 --> 00:09:37,034
The thing is,

140
00:09:37,118 --> 00:09:40,329
if anyone ends up dead,
we'll have a hell of a mess to deal with.

141
00:09:40,413 --> 00:09:41,747
What a moron.

142
00:09:41,831 --> 00:09:43,457
Don't make it look like I ordered it.

143
00:09:44,875 --> 00:09:47,336
Keep it quiet.
They're nothing but filthy nobodies.

144
00:09:49,005 --> 00:09:51,716
I shouldn't have bought that stupid land.

145
00:09:52,341 --> 00:09:53,676
Don't they get wildfires there?

146
00:09:53,759 --> 00:09:55,803
If a fire burned the place to the ground,

147
00:09:55,886 --> 00:09:57,096
that'd make my day.

148
00:09:57,638 --> 00:09:59,849
Round up some thugs to drive them out.

149
00:09:59,932 --> 00:10:01,142
Yes, sir.

150
00:10:05,605 --> 00:10:06,439
Sir.

151
00:10:07,440 --> 00:10:08,399
You have a visitor.

152
00:10:09,442 --> 00:10:10,484
A visitor?

153
00:10:13,237 --> 00:10:14,238
So you're saying

154
00:10:14,989 --> 00:10:18,034
you want to buy the Gyodang-ri
shantytown site from me?

155
00:10:19,827 --> 00:10:20,661
That's right.

156
00:10:20,745 --> 00:10:21,954
Well, well.

157
00:10:23,372 --> 00:10:27,460
My men did mention
they'd seen you at that filthy dump.

158
00:10:27,543 --> 00:10:29,920
Does your father know about this?

159
00:10:30,004 --> 00:10:30,921
No, sir.

160
00:10:31,422 --> 00:10:34,342
He doesn't even know I've come to see you.

161
00:10:35,635 --> 00:10:36,469
Well…

162
00:10:37,303 --> 00:10:39,513
I don't know
what's going on between you two,

163
00:10:40,014 --> 00:10:43,392
but I don't want to get
caught up in your feud with your father.

164
00:10:43,476 --> 00:10:44,560
You should leave.

165
00:10:45,186 --> 00:10:47,521
I'm a businessman, young man.

166
00:10:48,022 --> 00:10:51,067
Those beggars in Gyodang-ri
are a headache,

167
00:10:51,150 --> 00:10:55,946
but why would I give up a prime piece
of land I paid 400,000 won for?

168
00:10:56,614 --> 00:10:58,866
I could build modern-style houses there

169
00:10:58,949 --> 00:11:01,952
and rake in a fortune selling them off.

170
00:11:02,953 --> 00:11:04,705
You didn't buy the land

171
00:11:05,790 --> 00:11:07,792
just to sell modern-style houses.

172
00:11:08,959 --> 00:11:09,794
What?

173
00:11:09,877 --> 00:11:13,798
With the League of Nations Commission
coming soon, you bought Gyodang-ri

174
00:11:13,881 --> 00:11:16,592
to raze it and get it out
of the Government-General's way.

175
00:11:18,052 --> 00:11:19,970
With the inspector general in the family,

176
00:11:20,930 --> 00:11:23,140
the Government-General
will stand behind you.

177
00:11:23,224 --> 00:11:25,476
Joil Textile Factory's future
will be brighter than ever.

178
00:11:28,562 --> 00:11:29,438
Compared to that,

179
00:11:30,398 --> 00:11:32,400
the profit from the modern-style houses

180
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:34,151
is just pocket change.

181
00:11:34,819 --> 00:11:36,112
But as you know,

182
00:11:37,405 --> 00:11:38,572
things have taken a turn.

183
00:11:39,657 --> 00:11:41,951
Because of the recent incidents
caused by the subversives,

184
00:11:43,828 --> 00:11:45,329
my father will soon lose his post.

185
00:11:49,166 --> 00:11:51,502
Then all your grand plans will come down

186
00:11:52,545 --> 00:11:55,131
to just one round
of selling modern-style houses.

187
00:11:55,715 --> 00:11:59,093
But if you abandon that rotten rope
and take hold of a new one,

188
00:11:59,802 --> 00:12:02,513
the long-term rewards will far outweigh

189
00:12:03,931 --> 00:12:06,976
the profits from the Gyodang-ri
development, don't you think?

190
00:12:11,439 --> 00:12:12,273
So tell me,

191
00:12:13,065 --> 00:12:15,484
why do you want to buy
the land in Gyodang-ri?

192
00:12:16,235 --> 00:12:17,319
As you know,

193
00:12:18,446 --> 00:12:19,780
I was born here in Joseon.

194
00:12:20,614 --> 00:12:22,575
I wouldn't have much of a future in Japan.

195
00:12:25,411 --> 00:12:27,204
I want to make a big score

196
00:12:27,288 --> 00:12:28,831
before my father's influence runs out.

197
00:12:28,914 --> 00:12:32,376
My, my. I had no idea
you were a greedy little schemer.

198
00:12:32,460 --> 00:12:33,294
Fine.

199
00:12:34,211 --> 00:12:35,045
Can you afford it?

200
00:12:35,129 --> 00:12:37,423
I'm still the inspector general's son,

201
00:12:38,549 --> 00:12:41,177
so there are some
generous people willing to bet on me.

202
00:12:44,889 --> 00:12:46,098
I'll give you 500,000 won,

203
00:12:47,099 --> 00:12:49,727
along with what you want most.

204
00:12:50,728 --> 00:12:52,271
What I want most?

205
00:12:54,106 --> 00:12:55,065
What would that be?

206
00:12:57,026 --> 00:12:58,110
Calling off the engagement.

207
00:13:04,742 --> 00:13:08,078
Things got sorted out
fast thanks to you, Chief.

208
00:13:10,331 --> 00:13:11,207
Chief.

209
00:13:13,834 --> 00:13:14,668
One moment.

210
00:13:16,670 --> 00:13:17,630
What is it?

211
00:13:18,923 --> 00:13:20,758
Yoo Philip and his wife
have left their house.

212
00:13:21,509 --> 00:13:22,343
What?

213
00:13:22,426 --> 00:13:23,886
They left their house?

214
00:13:33,270 --> 00:13:34,355
Here, shake hands.

215
00:13:34,438 --> 00:13:35,689
Me too!

216
00:13:35,773 --> 00:13:37,525
I want to do it too!

217
00:13:37,608 --> 00:13:38,859
Me too.

218
00:13:38,943 --> 00:13:40,152
- Me too.
- Me too.

219
00:13:51,664 --> 00:13:52,498
So-ran.

220
00:13:57,795 --> 00:13:58,921
Does it look okay?

221
00:14:16,605 --> 00:14:17,690
- Welcome.
- Hello.

222
00:14:17,773 --> 00:14:18,607
Good to see you.

223
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:23,612
- I'd like to get these developed.
- Sure.

224
00:14:34,498 --> 00:14:35,624
- Hello, sir.
- Hello, sir.

225
00:14:44,675 --> 00:14:46,886
Search everywhere.
Check under the pillows too!

226
00:14:46,969 --> 00:14:48,178
- Yes, sir!
- Yes, sir!

227
00:14:51,765 --> 00:14:52,641
Sir.

228
00:14:55,853 --> 00:14:57,146
We found something in the study.

229
00:14:57,229 --> 00:14:58,647
It's an early-morning telegram

230
00:14:58,731 --> 00:15:00,900
from the Tokyo branch
of Yoo Philip's newspaper.

231
00:15:01,483 --> 00:15:03,152
I got it from the mail carrier

232
00:15:03,235 --> 00:15:04,862
and delivered it to Yoo Philip.

233
00:15:08,782 --> 00:15:10,868
The newspaper's Tokyo branch?

234
00:15:17,791 --> 00:15:21,545
TURMOIL TO END WITH INSPECTOR GENERAL
RETAINING POST, POLICE CHIEF RESIGNING

235
00:15:24,715 --> 00:15:28,093
These so-called journalists
believe these baseless rumors?

236
00:15:29,553 --> 00:15:31,805
Absolutely pathetic.

237
00:15:33,974 --> 00:15:35,893
Put it back where you found it.

238
00:15:35,976 --> 00:15:36,894
Yes, sir!

239
00:15:37,978 --> 00:15:39,021
That's enough.

240
00:15:39,605 --> 00:15:41,148
Return to your posts, all of you.

241
00:15:41,231 --> 00:15:43,192
- Yes, sir!
- Yes, sir!

242
00:15:54,620 --> 00:15:57,414
You're really going to dump
all the blame on me?

243
00:16:25,484 --> 00:16:27,194
They should've found it by now.

244
00:16:35,619 --> 00:16:38,831
I told you to report everything
you see and hear in the secretariat.

245
00:16:38,914 --> 00:16:41,333
Go on, let's hear it.

246
00:16:42,835 --> 00:16:45,713
There's one thing I heard.

247
00:16:46,255 --> 00:16:48,757
I'm not sure if it'll be useful to you.

248
00:16:50,467 --> 00:16:51,677
That's for me to decide.

249
00:16:51,760 --> 00:16:52,928
What is it? Spit it out.

250
00:16:53,012 --> 00:16:55,681
President Kim Seung-eok
of Joil Textile Factory

251
00:16:55,764 --> 00:16:59,268
came to see the inspector general,
and they had a huge argument.

252
00:17:00,310 --> 00:17:03,480
It was over something
called "a marriage contract."

253
00:17:04,064 --> 00:17:05,024
"A marriage contract"?

254
00:17:05,107 --> 00:17:06,191
Yes.

255
00:17:06,275 --> 00:17:09,528
I couldn't hear everything
with Secretary Wada around,

256
00:17:10,029 --> 00:17:12,906
but I definitely heard the words
"marriage contract."

257
00:17:20,080 --> 00:17:21,707
So that's what it was all about.

258
00:17:23,250 --> 00:17:26,211
He kept bringing up the past
to lecture me.

259
00:17:26,295 --> 00:17:27,796
But it was all just an excuse!

260
00:17:30,799 --> 00:17:34,053
There's dirty money between them,
so he's let Kim Seung-eok push him around.

261
00:17:37,306 --> 00:17:38,140
Lee Ga-kyung.

262
00:17:39,391 --> 00:17:41,185
Go steal that marriage contract.

263
00:17:41,769 --> 00:17:42,603
Sorry?

264
00:17:43,812 --> 00:17:44,730
Me?

265
00:17:44,813 --> 00:17:45,647
Yes.

266
00:17:46,440 --> 00:17:49,651
There's only one place
the inspector general would keep that.

267
00:17:49,735 --> 00:17:51,487
It's the cabinet in his own office.

268
00:17:52,613 --> 00:17:54,031
Get in and steal it tonight.

269
00:17:55,240 --> 00:17:56,241
Tonight?

270
00:17:57,910 --> 00:17:59,787
Don't make me repeat myself.

271
00:17:59,870 --> 00:18:00,788
Tonight.

272
00:18:00,871 --> 00:18:03,499
The governor-general
leaves for Tokyo in three days.

273
00:18:04,083 --> 00:18:05,959
How am I supposed to steal it?

274
00:18:07,711 --> 00:18:09,588
Isn't stealing your specialty?

275
00:18:11,131 --> 00:18:12,424
But things are different.

276
00:18:13,217 --> 00:18:16,470
Secretary Wada keeps an eye on me all day,
so I can barely move.

277
00:18:16,553 --> 00:18:17,387
And at night,

278
00:18:17,471 --> 00:18:19,765
even after the inspector general
leaves for the day,

279
00:18:19,848 --> 00:18:22,184
there are guards outside his office.

280
00:18:31,485 --> 00:18:33,821
I'll get the night guards out of the way.

281
00:18:36,990 --> 00:18:37,825
Right.

282
00:18:38,700 --> 00:18:39,535
Besides,

283
00:18:42,454 --> 00:18:43,789
you should be more worried

284
00:18:43,872 --> 00:18:46,166
about coming back empty-handed
than getting caught.

285
00:19:05,060 --> 00:19:06,562
The night guards are taken care of.

286
00:19:08,522 --> 00:19:10,440
Now I just need to pick the lock.

287
00:19:13,527 --> 00:19:18,448
<i>- If I had a million won</i>
<i>- If I had a million won</i>

288
00:19:18,532 --> 00:19:22,578
<i>- I'd buy a gold hairpin and a fancy ring</i>
<i>- A fancy ring</i>

289
00:19:22,661 --> 00:19:24,538
No-ma, can I ask you something?

290
00:19:24,621 --> 00:19:25,455
Yeah.

291
00:19:25,539 --> 00:19:30,210
Lots of grown-ups and older kids
put their thumbprints on that paper.

292
00:19:30,294 --> 00:19:32,963
Does that mean
we won't get kicked out anymore?

293
00:19:33,046 --> 00:19:35,257
Can we stay here?

294
00:19:35,340 --> 00:19:38,218
Of course. They won't kick us out
if we show them the petitions.

295
00:19:38,302 --> 00:19:39,178
Don't worry.

296
00:19:45,058 --> 00:19:45,893
Hold on.

297
00:19:47,936 --> 00:19:49,813
Kkot-nim, wait right here, okay?

298
00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:52,274
What? Why?

299
00:19:57,863 --> 00:19:59,406
Boss, when did you get here?

300
00:20:01,491 --> 00:20:03,577
Hey, what's all this?

301
00:20:05,120 --> 00:20:06,705
Nothing. It's just trash.

302
00:20:06,788 --> 00:20:08,916
I found them lying around,
so I picked them up.

303
00:20:12,377 --> 00:20:13,253
You picked them up?

304
00:20:14,671 --> 00:20:15,672
What for?

305
00:20:17,966 --> 00:20:20,302
To use in the outhouse.

306
00:20:20,385 --> 00:20:22,304
Kkot-nim's constipation--

307
00:20:24,890 --> 00:20:26,725
Boss, what's wrong?

308
00:20:29,228 --> 00:20:30,729
What? Toilet paper?

309
00:20:32,147 --> 00:20:33,065
Constipation?

310
00:20:33,148 --> 00:20:35,484
You've been running around

311
00:20:35,567 --> 00:20:37,903
the whole neighborhood with these papers.

312
00:20:37,986 --> 00:20:39,780
You think I didn't know that?

313
00:20:39,863 --> 00:20:41,490
Why would you stick your nose into this?

314
00:20:41,573 --> 00:20:43,075
What makes you think you can?

315
00:20:44,117 --> 00:20:45,869
You keep asking for a beating.

316
00:20:47,746 --> 00:20:48,580
Lee No-ma.

317
00:20:49,164 --> 00:20:50,582
Get a grip.

318
00:20:51,208 --> 00:20:53,710
What do you think
this stupid paper can do?

319
00:20:53,794 --> 00:20:56,255
Your parents didn't know
how the world worked.

320
00:20:56,338 --> 00:20:58,674
They died shouting
for independence, all for nothing.

321
00:20:58,757 --> 00:21:01,677
Does that stupidity run in your family?

322
00:21:03,011 --> 00:21:05,514
Do you think
this is going to change anything?

323
00:21:05,597 --> 00:21:07,057
Not a chance!

324
00:21:08,475 --> 00:21:09,351
Fine.

325
00:21:12,562 --> 00:21:13,480
You won't let this go

326
00:21:14,106 --> 00:21:15,482
until I kill you, will you?

327
00:21:19,945 --> 00:21:21,530
Damn it, who the hell did that?

328
00:21:21,613 --> 00:21:23,824
Stop hitting No-ma!

329
00:21:23,907 --> 00:21:25,325
Don't hit him!

330
00:21:25,951 --> 00:21:27,869
- I said stop hitting him!
- Don't beat him!

331
00:21:27,953 --> 00:21:29,329
- Stop it!
- Leave him alone!

332
00:21:29,413 --> 00:21:30,789
Stop it.

333
00:21:30,872 --> 00:21:31,915
I said stop.

334
00:21:31,999 --> 00:21:32,958
Stop beating him!

335
00:21:35,335 --> 00:21:36,586
Are you out of your damn minds?

336
00:21:36,670 --> 00:21:38,255
That's enough, Mun-mak!

337
00:21:48,974 --> 00:21:50,183
What the hell?

338
00:21:51,226 --> 00:21:52,769
So you've all come together.

339
00:21:53,645 --> 00:21:54,896
What, you think that scares me?

340
00:21:55,731 --> 00:21:57,983
Do you all want to end up like him?

341
00:21:58,066 --> 00:21:59,234
Hey, Mun-mak.

342
00:22:00,235 --> 00:22:04,072
Did you think we'd let you
walk all over us forever?

343
00:22:06,742 --> 00:22:09,536
Is this old geezer senile or what?

344
00:22:13,415 --> 00:22:15,125
We've had enough.

345
00:22:15,208 --> 00:22:16,626
We won't put up with this anymore!

346
00:22:20,547 --> 00:22:23,800
You know, even a worm
fights back when stepped on.

347
00:22:23,884 --> 00:22:27,554
You can cut it up,
but it'll still come back to life!

348
00:22:27,637 --> 00:22:29,765
You bastard!
You're worse than Lee Wan-yong!

349
00:22:30,265 --> 00:22:31,600
Oh no, No-ma.

350
00:22:31,683 --> 00:22:33,018
Help him up.

351
00:22:33,602 --> 00:22:35,062
Can you get up?

352
00:22:35,145 --> 00:22:36,063
There you go.

353
00:22:45,989 --> 00:22:47,032
Take that.

354
00:22:51,370 --> 00:22:52,829
Here, hold this.

355
00:22:53,455 --> 00:22:55,165
You need an excuse to be here.

356
00:22:56,249 --> 00:22:57,084
Thanks.

357
00:22:58,335 --> 00:23:00,253
Well… listen.

358
00:23:00,921 --> 00:23:05,884
I wanted to thank you
for saving my life back then.

359
00:23:07,928 --> 00:23:09,179
I should be thanking you.

360
00:23:09,262 --> 00:23:12,015
Thanks to you,
I finally got some decent rest.

361
00:23:17,354 --> 00:23:19,940
Everything's going according to plan.

362
00:23:21,191 --> 00:23:23,902
Fukuda told me to sneak
into the Inspector General's Office

363
00:23:23,985 --> 00:23:25,278
and steal the contract tonight.

364
00:23:25,946 --> 00:23:28,240
Once Fukuda gets that marriage contract,

365
00:23:28,323 --> 00:23:31,535
he'll go straight to the governor-general
and tear Sato apart.

366
00:23:33,412 --> 00:23:35,122
Can you handle it on your own?

367
00:23:36,832 --> 00:23:37,999
How can I help you?

368
00:23:39,835 --> 00:23:41,878
I'll be fine. I can handle it.

369
00:23:41,962 --> 00:23:43,713
Picking a lock is easy.

370
00:23:48,176 --> 00:23:49,886
To be honest, I'm a little scared.

371
00:23:52,097 --> 00:23:55,392
I've been scared plenty of times
at the Government-General.

372
00:23:56,143 --> 00:23:58,145
But I've never been this scared.

373
00:24:02,649 --> 00:24:06,570
How have you managed
to work as a spy for over a decade?

374
00:24:09,948 --> 00:24:12,200
I was so busy that
I never got around to quitting.

375
00:24:16,329 --> 00:24:17,539
So that's why.

376
00:24:18,123 --> 00:24:19,374
Still, I'm glad

377
00:24:20,292 --> 00:24:23,044
you won't have to be a spy
as long as I have.

378
00:24:30,343 --> 00:24:31,219
Sometimes,

379
00:24:32,679 --> 00:24:36,558
I get chills when I realize I'm more used
to being called Suzuki than Hong Seog-won.

380
00:24:37,601 --> 00:24:38,727
At times like that,

381
00:24:38,810 --> 00:24:41,563
I've wanted to give up
but couldn't bring myself to quit.

382
00:24:42,939 --> 00:24:44,399
Because then, all those deaths

383
00:24:45,317 --> 00:24:46,735
might have been for nothing.

384
00:24:50,947 --> 00:24:52,240
I'm on edge every single day.

385
00:24:53,325 --> 00:24:55,702
Even after more than a decade
at the Government-General,

386
00:24:55,785 --> 00:24:57,287
every moment is nerve-racking.

387
00:24:58,830 --> 00:24:59,706
So…

388
00:25:04,419 --> 00:25:05,795
finish this quickly and go home.

389
00:25:06,463 --> 00:25:09,382
Don't stay so long
that you lose your own name, like I have.

390
00:25:31,780 --> 00:25:32,822
Where am I?

391
00:25:34,741 --> 00:25:35,659
My head hurts.

392
00:25:36,785 --> 00:25:37,702
What's this thing?

393
00:25:38,787 --> 00:25:39,913
Damn thing looks ridiculous.

394
00:25:41,164 --> 00:25:41,998
Wait.

395
00:25:42,082 --> 00:25:43,708
You shouldn't take that bandage off.

396
00:25:45,460 --> 00:25:46,294
And who are you?

397
00:25:46,920 --> 00:25:47,879
Let me have a look.

398
00:25:48,755 --> 00:25:49,631
Here, let me see.

399
00:25:50,840 --> 00:25:52,884
Good, the bleeding stopped.

400
00:25:52,968 --> 00:25:54,344
If the bandage bothers you,

401
00:25:54,427 --> 00:25:56,137
use the ointment I'll give you.

402
00:25:57,138 --> 00:26:00,225
People found you passed out
outside the tavern and brought you here.

403
00:26:00,308 --> 00:26:02,811
You got hurt. Why'd you drink so much?

404
00:26:04,312 --> 00:26:05,772
That's none of your business.

405
00:26:06,648 --> 00:26:07,732
Just give me some money.

406
00:26:07,816 --> 00:26:08,900
Excuse me?

407
00:26:08,984 --> 00:26:11,069
You brought me here without asking.

408
00:26:11,653 --> 00:26:13,029
You should take responsibility.

409
00:26:13,655 --> 00:26:14,864
You're something else.

410
00:26:17,075 --> 00:26:19,703
I'll give you enough
for a bowl of <i>seolleongtang.</i>

411
00:26:19,786 --> 00:26:20,620
Wait here.

412
00:26:58,742 --> 00:27:02,287
I put it there
so the police wouldn't get suspicious.

413
00:27:04,456 --> 00:27:05,540
That makes sense.

414
00:27:06,124 --> 00:27:07,083
Good thinking.

415
00:27:10,879 --> 00:27:11,880
Then,

416
00:27:11,963 --> 00:27:14,049
I'll bring it back.

417
00:27:14,633 --> 00:27:15,550
Please do.

418
00:27:21,973 --> 00:27:23,683
Did you have a good day out today?

419
00:27:25,352 --> 00:27:26,853
Not the operation. Just today.

420
00:27:30,440 --> 00:27:31,274
Yes,

421
00:27:32,484 --> 00:27:33,526
I had a good time.

422
00:27:36,154 --> 00:27:36,988
Me too.

423
00:27:41,618 --> 00:27:42,452
So-ran.

424
00:27:45,455 --> 00:27:47,207
I know this isn't the time to say this…

425
00:27:48,166 --> 00:27:49,584
Excuse me.

426
00:27:50,210 --> 00:27:51,336
I need to go to the kitchen.

427
00:28:03,765 --> 00:28:05,266
After we kill Sato…

428
00:28:10,480 --> 00:28:12,816
let's go on a real date.
Not as part of an operation.

429
00:28:33,211 --> 00:28:36,464
<i>I'll delay the night guards' shift</i>
<i>outside the Inspector General's Office.</i>

430
00:28:37,048 --> 00:28:38,758
<i>I can't hold them off for long. Move fast.</i>

431
00:29:11,958 --> 00:29:12,792
Darn it.

432
00:29:13,293 --> 00:29:14,586
Why isn't it opening?

433
00:29:14,669 --> 00:29:15,795
Is it made in America?

434
00:29:23,261 --> 00:29:24,095
There.

435
00:29:54,542 --> 00:29:56,211
Father's back at the Government-General.

436
00:30:01,424 --> 00:30:02,342
Inspector General!

437
00:30:08,932 --> 00:30:10,266
You need to get out of here.

438
00:30:10,850 --> 00:30:11,684
Wait.

439
00:30:11,768 --> 00:30:12,727
I've unlocked it.

440
00:30:13,561 --> 00:30:14,771
Please buy me some time.

441
00:30:37,961 --> 00:30:38,795
Hideo.

442
00:30:43,633 --> 00:30:44,551
Father.

443
00:30:51,975 --> 00:30:52,809
Father.

444
00:30:53,434 --> 00:30:54,978
What are you doing here at this hour?

445
00:30:55,061 --> 00:30:58,189
I came to show you the draft
of your speech for the farewell ceremony.

446
00:31:01,401 --> 00:31:02,986
Were there no guards at the door?

447
00:31:03,486 --> 00:31:05,238
You mean the night guards?

448
00:31:06,364 --> 00:31:09,117
It seems the secretary forgot
to call them when you left for the day.

449
00:31:09,701 --> 00:31:10,618
I'll contact them.

450
00:31:14,122 --> 00:31:16,374
Are you working late again?

451
00:31:16,457 --> 00:31:17,292
No.

452
00:31:18,167 --> 00:31:20,795
I was heading home
when I realized I'd forgotten something.

453
00:31:21,754 --> 00:31:24,215
Good timing.
The Commission's farewell ceremony--

454
00:31:24,299 --> 00:31:25,466
Hold that thought.

455
00:32:09,677 --> 00:32:10,637
What's wrong?

456
00:33:12,115 --> 00:33:13,491
COMMISSION'S FAREWELL SPEECH DRAFT

457
00:33:25,920 --> 00:33:29,507
Make absolutely sure everything's
ready for the Commission's arrival.

458
00:33:29,590 --> 00:33:31,300
There must be no room for error.

459
00:33:33,219 --> 00:33:35,805
The wedding will go ahead
as planned, just so you know.

460
00:33:40,143 --> 00:33:41,102
I don't want to.

461
00:33:44,439 --> 00:33:45,356
You don't want to?

462
00:34:10,256 --> 00:34:12,550
Does it look like
I'm asking what you want?

463
00:34:13,843 --> 00:34:15,470
What if I refuse to the very end?

464
00:34:15,553 --> 00:34:16,512
In that case,

465
00:34:17,847 --> 00:34:21,267
I'll assume it's because of Keum Seo-hee.

466
00:34:24,020 --> 00:34:26,105
I should have gotten rid of her long ago.

467
00:34:26,689 --> 00:34:28,024
I've been far too patient.

468
00:34:28,107 --> 00:34:30,109
This has nothing to do with Ms. Keum.

469
00:34:30,193 --> 00:34:31,486
No, it doesn't.

470
00:34:31,569 --> 00:34:33,780
But none of that matters anymore.

471
00:34:35,073 --> 00:34:36,032
What matters is

472
00:34:37,283 --> 00:34:39,494
that you don't do anything stupid.

473
00:34:41,537 --> 00:34:43,706
You're still on the Gugukdan's side.

474
00:34:44,373 --> 00:34:45,792
You think I don't know that?

475
00:34:50,546 --> 00:34:52,465
But you're my son, Hideo.

476
00:34:54,509 --> 00:34:55,676
That's your misfortune.

477
00:34:57,637 --> 00:34:59,388
It could be yours.

478
00:35:01,474 --> 00:35:04,519
If word gets out that the Joseon boy
you adopted, raised, and indoctrinated

479
00:35:04,602 --> 00:35:06,604
has joined the independence movement,

480
00:35:07,897 --> 00:35:09,857
it won't bode well for your future.

481
00:35:13,486 --> 00:35:15,113
Could you handle that?

482
00:35:17,115 --> 00:35:19,742
Watching everything
you've worked so hard to build

483
00:35:21,202 --> 00:35:23,746
come crashing down
because of one adopted Joseon son?

484
00:35:25,498 --> 00:35:26,332
I intend

485
00:35:27,625 --> 00:35:29,627
to become the governor-general of Joseon.

486
00:35:32,046 --> 00:35:35,133
From here, I'll make my way
to mainland Japan and climb even higher.

487
00:35:36,926 --> 00:35:39,679
You think a few bombs from the Gugukdan

488
00:35:39,762 --> 00:35:41,389
can really stop me?

489
00:35:41,472 --> 00:35:42,306
No.

490
00:35:43,641 --> 00:35:45,268
Nobody can stop me.

491
00:35:46,727 --> 00:35:50,273
I'll cut down anyone who stands in my way…

492
00:35:52,859 --> 00:35:54,235
even if it's you.

493
00:35:55,695 --> 00:35:57,613
So don't do anything reckless, Hideo.

494
00:35:58,656 --> 00:36:00,283
Wherever your heart lies

495
00:36:01,117 --> 00:36:03,244
or whatever subversive dreams
you may harbor,

496
00:36:05,163 --> 00:36:06,205
I won't mind.

497
00:36:08,124 --> 00:36:10,168
But the moment
you make a move toward them,

498
00:36:13,004 --> 00:36:13,838
Keum Seo-hee…

499
00:36:16,048 --> 00:36:17,175
is done for.

500
00:36:32,940 --> 00:36:35,318
He took a bribe in the form of a dowry?

501
00:36:36,235 --> 00:36:39,447
So you'll bury my past now?

502
00:36:40,031 --> 00:36:41,490
Looks like it's all over for you,

503
00:36:42,909 --> 00:36:44,160
Inspector General.

504
00:36:45,244 --> 00:36:46,871
Excuse me, sir.

505
00:36:47,455 --> 00:36:48,289
Get lost.

506
00:37:23,783 --> 00:37:24,825
Ms. Hanako.

507
00:37:24,909 --> 00:37:26,994
It's bedtime.

508
00:37:27,703 --> 00:37:30,248
Ms. Hanako, please go to your room.

509
00:37:30,331 --> 00:37:31,707
Okay.

510
00:37:32,291 --> 00:37:35,086
You're not going to wait
for your brother today?

511
00:37:37,505 --> 00:37:39,966
Did you know he searched

512
00:37:40,049 --> 00:37:45,429
every shop in Keijo
to find your new wheelchair?

513
00:37:46,013 --> 00:37:47,640
Listen, Yanagi.

514
00:37:48,182 --> 00:37:49,725
I mean, Ms. Yanagi.

515
00:37:49,809 --> 00:37:50,643
Yes?

516
00:37:52,353 --> 00:37:54,313
When Hideo gets married,

517
00:37:55,523 --> 00:37:58,359
you're not going to go
live with him, are you?

518
00:38:01,821 --> 00:38:03,572
Don't go anywhere.

519
00:38:06,284 --> 00:38:08,369
Stay here with me.

520
00:38:09,328 --> 00:38:10,246
Please.

521
00:38:11,289 --> 00:38:12,373
I…

522
00:38:14,083 --> 00:38:16,460
I don't want to live alone

523
00:38:17,086 --> 00:38:17,962
with Father.

524
00:38:26,470 --> 00:38:27,471
If…

525
00:38:31,559 --> 00:38:32,935
by any chance…

526
00:38:35,563 --> 00:38:37,315
something were to happen to me…

527
00:38:58,252 --> 00:38:59,378
Don't worry.

528
00:39:01,422 --> 00:39:02,590
I won't go anywhere.

529
00:39:04,467 --> 00:39:06,093
I'll keep my promise.

530
00:39:07,386 --> 00:39:09,055
What did you just say?

531
00:39:12,308 --> 00:39:14,935
Learn the Joseon language
if you want to know.

532
00:39:16,228 --> 00:39:20,066
What? Tell me what you just said.

533
00:39:20,149 --> 00:39:22,818
Time for bed, Ms. Hanako.

534
00:39:23,319 --> 00:39:24,362
I don't want to.

535
00:39:24,445 --> 00:39:28,240
I'm not going to sleep
until you tell me what you said.

536
00:39:30,659 --> 00:39:31,952
Here are 50 bullets

537
00:39:32,036 --> 00:39:35,373
smuggled in from China,
disguised as camera film.

538
00:39:35,456 --> 00:39:38,334
Mr. Kim said they handed over the bullets
without hesitation

539
00:39:38,417 --> 00:39:40,127
when he told them it was for the Gugukdan.

540
00:39:41,837 --> 00:39:44,382
I got them from the newspaper office,
where they'd been hidden.

541
00:39:45,174 --> 00:39:46,175
Good work.

542
00:39:53,891 --> 00:39:55,309
Let's store them downstairs.

543
00:40:14,954 --> 00:40:15,788
What the?

544
00:40:16,872 --> 00:40:18,374
Is this a Gugukdan hideout?

545
00:40:33,597 --> 00:40:34,432
What?

546
00:40:41,188 --> 00:40:42,189
Look at these little…

547
00:40:47,278 --> 00:40:50,114
MYEONGWOLGWAN

548
00:40:52,575 --> 00:40:57,538
Your father will be furious
if he finds out.

549
00:40:57,621 --> 00:41:00,416
I'll take care of it. Don't worry.

550
00:41:01,375 --> 00:41:02,626
Calling off the engagement too?

551
00:41:02,710 --> 00:41:05,504
This marriage was arranged
between our families,

552
00:41:05,588 --> 00:41:07,047
but it's still my marriage.

553
00:41:07,131 --> 00:41:07,965
Leave it to me.

554
00:41:12,636 --> 00:41:14,013
You may not be

555
00:41:14,638 --> 00:41:16,390
the right man to marry my daughter,

556
00:41:17,224 --> 00:41:20,603
but you're certainly good enough
for a young lady from a well-off family.

557
00:41:21,645 --> 00:41:22,646
What do you say?

558
00:41:22,730 --> 00:41:24,482
Shall I play matchmaker?

559
00:41:26,150 --> 00:41:27,985
That's very kind of you.

560
00:41:29,904 --> 00:41:31,655
But I already have someone in mind.

561
00:41:39,830 --> 00:41:40,998
Your guest is here.

562
00:41:42,124 --> 00:41:43,209
I believe she's here.

563
00:41:55,387 --> 00:41:56,722
Allow me to introduce her.

564
00:41:57,306 --> 00:41:58,349
This is my investor,

565
00:41:59,683 --> 00:42:00,893
Ms. Hosokawa.

566
00:42:33,592 --> 00:42:34,843
Thank you for helping me.

567
00:42:35,469 --> 00:42:37,346
I did it because you asked,

568
00:42:38,138 --> 00:42:40,224
but tell me you're not doing
anything dangerous.

569
00:42:40,307 --> 00:42:41,767
It's not dangerous.

570
00:42:43,185 --> 00:42:44,061
It is

571
00:42:45,062 --> 00:42:46,188
the right thing to do.

572
00:42:50,693 --> 00:42:52,695
You asked what I would do when my father

573
00:42:53,571 --> 00:42:55,030
returns to Japan, didn't you?

574
00:42:57,408 --> 00:42:58,909
I'll stay in Joseon.

575
00:42:59,743 --> 00:43:00,953
I want to find my mother…

576
00:43:04,248 --> 00:43:05,666
and live as a Joseon person.

577
00:43:07,585 --> 00:43:08,419
Hideo.

578
00:43:11,130 --> 00:43:12,006
I'm Tae-ung.

579
00:43:14,842 --> 00:43:17,511
Please call me Kim Tae-ung from now on.

580
00:43:18,762 --> 00:43:20,014
My real name.

581
00:43:23,058 --> 00:43:23,892
All right.

582
00:43:31,358 --> 00:43:32,276
Tae-ung.

583
00:43:41,744 --> 00:43:43,871
You filled the void my mother left behind.

584
00:43:48,167 --> 00:43:49,126
I appreciate that.

585
00:43:50,919 --> 00:43:53,130
I still remember
how to speak the Joseon language,

586
00:43:54,381 --> 00:43:55,591
thanks to you.

587
00:44:03,015 --> 00:44:04,224
Tae-ung.

588
00:44:05,768 --> 00:44:07,478
There's something I want you to know.

589
00:44:08,354 --> 00:44:09,188
What is it?

590
00:44:14,443 --> 00:44:16,528
Later, when we're home.

591
00:44:17,321 --> 00:44:18,614
I'll tell you at home tonight.

592
00:44:20,532 --> 00:44:21,825
Okay, I understand.

593
00:44:24,912 --> 00:44:26,955
I think I should go now.

594
00:44:27,665 --> 00:44:28,791
Sorry I can't take you home.

595
00:44:28,874 --> 00:44:30,084
It's okay. Off you go.

596
00:44:36,965 --> 00:44:37,883
Ae-yeong.

597
00:44:40,427 --> 00:44:41,470
He's grown into

598
00:44:42,888 --> 00:44:44,431
such a fine young man.

599
00:45:05,244 --> 00:45:06,745
You need to tell me now.

600
00:45:07,413 --> 00:45:10,290
How did you come up with
the 150,000 won we were short?

601
00:45:12,751 --> 00:45:14,420
I forged Dad's power of attorney…

602
00:45:16,380 --> 00:45:18,340
so I could take the gold bars
from the bank vault.

603
00:45:19,466 --> 00:45:22,302
Even after I'd sold my jewelry
and you'd taken the factory funds,

604
00:45:22,886 --> 00:45:24,096
we didn't have enough.

605
00:45:24,179 --> 00:45:26,306
But what if you'd been caught?

606
00:45:28,851 --> 00:45:31,019
You would've tried to stop me if you knew.

607
00:45:31,103 --> 00:45:32,187
How could I tell you?

608
00:45:36,358 --> 00:45:37,192
I'm sorry.

609
00:45:37,943 --> 00:45:39,361
The bank vault is one thing,

610
00:45:39,987 --> 00:45:42,865
but in ten days, your father will notice
the factory funds are missing.

611
00:45:43,574 --> 00:45:45,451
That could put you in trouble.

612
00:45:46,410 --> 00:45:48,912
Let's make it look like
I embezzled the funds and fled.

613
00:45:49,621 --> 00:45:51,999
I'll join the Gugukdan headquarters
in Shanghai.

614
00:45:54,710 --> 00:45:55,669
I'm coming with you.

615
00:45:56,295 --> 00:45:57,129
To Shanghai.

616
00:46:00,090 --> 00:46:01,175
What?

617
00:46:02,092 --> 00:46:05,220
Do you think I'd open
my father's bank vault without a plan?

618
00:46:07,639 --> 00:46:09,308
I never got to answer you before.

619
00:46:11,560 --> 00:46:12,478
I have my answer now.

620
00:46:16,648 --> 00:46:17,983
Let's leave for Shanghai.

621
00:46:20,277 --> 00:46:21,111
Together.

622
00:46:30,454 --> 00:46:32,206
I'm in love with you, Young-bok.

623
00:46:38,462 --> 00:46:40,088
I've loved you for so long

624
00:46:41,006 --> 00:46:42,549
that I can't even remember…

625
00:46:45,844 --> 00:46:46,970
when it began.

626
00:47:16,291 --> 00:47:18,460
FAREWELL CEREMONY
FOR THE LEAGUE OF NATIONS COMMISSION

627
00:47:23,590 --> 00:47:26,426
You went to see
the foreign reporters to say you're back?

628
00:47:26,969 --> 00:47:27,886
What did they say?

629
00:47:27,970 --> 00:47:31,223
Some seemed a little unsettled
by the recent incidents,

630
00:47:31,306 --> 00:47:32,599
but it's nothing to worry about.

631
00:47:32,683 --> 00:47:35,936
They come swarming all over like flies
whenever something happens.

632
00:47:36,895 --> 00:47:38,021
What about Yoo Philip?

633
00:47:38,105 --> 00:47:42,442
I went to see him at his newspaper office
and later had dinner at his house.

634
00:47:42,526 --> 00:47:45,320
The couple was shaken
by the pneumatic tube explosion,

635
00:47:45,404 --> 00:47:47,281
so I assured them again

636
00:47:47,364 --> 00:47:48,782
it was a mechanical failure.

637
00:47:49,741 --> 00:47:51,660
The head of the Far East branch
of his newspaper

638
00:47:51,743 --> 00:47:54,454
is coming to Keijo
with the League of Nations Commission.

639
00:47:54,538 --> 00:47:56,081
I'll keep a close eye on him.

640
00:47:56,874 --> 00:47:57,833
"Keep a close eye."

641
00:47:58,750 --> 00:48:00,794
That won't be enough this time, Suzuki.

642
00:48:02,045 --> 00:48:03,714
I won't disappoint you, sir.

643
00:48:05,090 --> 00:48:06,174
That'll be all.

644
00:48:06,258 --> 00:48:07,426
Yes, sir.

645
00:48:12,472 --> 00:48:13,307
Sir.

646
00:48:14,850 --> 00:48:17,895
Work is important,
but you should take care of yourself too.

647
00:48:19,396 --> 00:48:20,564
You stayed late working

648
00:48:20,647 --> 00:48:23,775
with the chief of police again last night.

649
00:48:26,445 --> 00:48:27,321
You mean Fukuda?

650
00:48:27,404 --> 00:48:28,405
Yes.

651
00:48:28,488 --> 00:48:32,576
I saw him leaving
your office late last night.

652
00:48:33,952 --> 00:48:37,497
Everyone's concerned
you're working yourself too hard.

653
00:48:37,581 --> 00:48:40,500
Please go home early
and get some rest today.

654
00:48:54,181 --> 00:48:55,766
Were there no guards at the door?

655
00:50:05,419 --> 00:50:07,170
Inspector General's Office. Wada speaking.

656
00:50:10,048 --> 00:50:10,882
Sorry?

657
00:50:21,309 --> 00:50:22,394
Come in.

658
00:50:27,065 --> 00:50:27,983
Inspector General.

659
00:50:28,066 --> 00:50:31,153
His Excellency
is leaving for Tokyo right now.

660
00:50:31,236 --> 00:50:32,112
What?

661
00:50:32,696 --> 00:50:33,530
Right now?

662
00:50:33,613 --> 00:50:34,448
Yes.

663
00:50:34,531 --> 00:50:37,075
He's suddenly leaving
earlier than planned.

664
00:50:42,414 --> 00:50:43,957
Go on ahead.

665
00:50:44,041 --> 00:50:44,875
Yes, sir.

666
00:50:49,212 --> 00:50:51,131
What are you doing here?
I didn't call for you.

667
00:50:51,214 --> 00:50:53,508
The governor-general is leaving right now.

668
00:50:53,592 --> 00:50:54,718
What? Now?

669
00:50:55,677 --> 00:50:57,179
Why didn't you tell me sooner?

670
00:51:04,186 --> 00:51:05,771
You should be more worried

671
00:51:05,854 --> 00:51:08,356
about coming back empty-handed
than getting caught.

672
00:51:10,901 --> 00:51:11,985
<i>Fukuda.</i>

673
00:51:12,069 --> 00:51:13,945
<i>I'll make sure you're dealt with too.</i>

674
00:51:22,704 --> 00:51:23,538
Your Excellency!

675
00:51:32,214 --> 00:51:33,507
Good day, sir!

676
00:51:36,551 --> 00:51:39,721
I heard you were leaving
ahead of schedule,

677
00:51:39,805 --> 00:51:41,306
so I came to see you off, sir.

678
00:51:42,057 --> 00:51:43,475
Have a safe trip, sir.

679
00:51:43,558 --> 00:51:45,102
By the time you return,

680
00:51:45,185 --> 00:51:48,939
I promise everything will be settled
with the Gugukdan and Gyodang-ri.

681
00:51:50,857 --> 00:51:55,403
I'm heading to Tokyo now
because you failed to handle that.

682
00:52:12,712 --> 00:52:14,840
Shall we take a stroll while we're here?

683
00:52:15,507 --> 00:52:16,383
Sure.

684
00:52:25,308 --> 00:52:27,018
How long has it been

685
00:52:27,686 --> 00:52:28,687
since we first met?

686
00:52:29,437 --> 00:52:30,814
It's been 20 years.

687
00:52:32,524 --> 00:52:34,192
Has it been that long already?

688
00:52:35,402 --> 00:52:38,029
I suppose you're getting old, sir.

689
00:52:39,239 --> 00:52:41,116
Are you feeling nostalgic?

690
00:52:44,244 --> 00:52:46,329
More than usual today.

691
00:52:47,247 --> 00:52:48,790
It brings back memories.

692
00:52:49,958 --> 00:52:51,877
Do you remember Ono Fumio?

693
00:52:53,211 --> 00:52:55,046
When I was mayor of Osaka,

694
00:52:55,130 --> 00:52:56,882
he was the one I appointed deputy mayor.

695
00:52:57,507 --> 00:52:58,341
Yes.

696
00:52:59,134 --> 00:53:01,803
Wasn't he one of your loyal followers?

697
00:53:02,512 --> 00:53:04,931
I found out he'd been
making connections in Tokyo

698
00:53:05,515 --> 00:53:06,933
behind my back.

699
00:53:09,019 --> 00:53:11,897
It's like getting bitten
by the dog I raised.

700
00:53:13,231 --> 00:53:14,608
The nerve of him.

701
00:53:20,113 --> 00:53:22,032
What's more important is

702
00:53:23,575 --> 00:53:25,535
why the dog bit you, isn't it?

703
00:53:35,629 --> 00:53:39,007
It's a habit I've picked up
working in the Bureau of Police Affairs.

704
00:53:40,383 --> 00:53:43,386
I find myself wondering
why a traitor felt they had no choice

705
00:53:44,179 --> 00:53:45,639
but to betray their own side.

706
00:53:46,765 --> 00:53:48,475
Just a job-related habit.

707
00:53:49,434 --> 00:53:51,645
"Had no choice
but to betray their own side."

708
00:53:58,485 --> 00:54:02,572
What are you going to do about Gyodang-ri?

709
00:54:03,657 --> 00:54:06,368
I heard Kim Seung-eok sold that land.

710
00:54:09,162 --> 00:54:10,080
He sold it?

711
00:54:13,124 --> 00:54:14,834
How do you know that?

712
00:54:18,213 --> 00:54:21,424
I've learned a thing or two
about what's between you and him.

713
00:54:25,262 --> 00:54:27,389
Actually, quite a bit more

714
00:54:28,056 --> 00:54:29,516
than you'd think.

715
00:54:34,354 --> 00:54:35,313
I see.

716
00:54:41,152 --> 00:54:41,987
Yes.

717
00:55:07,721 --> 00:55:08,763
Let's go now.

718
00:55:11,474 --> 00:55:12,767
Looks like you've lost a button.

719
00:55:15,729 --> 00:55:16,813
Thank you.

720
00:55:26,323 --> 00:55:27,198
Open the gate.

721
00:55:31,995 --> 00:55:33,038
There it is.

722
00:55:33,955 --> 00:55:35,915
I might as well
sell it for what it's worth.

723
00:55:42,088 --> 00:55:44,215
My goodness, I'm dying here.

724
00:55:44,799 --> 00:55:45,967
- Sorry!
- Get up already!

725
00:55:47,385 --> 00:55:50,096
I'm Ma Mun-mak from Gyodang-ri!

726
00:55:50,180 --> 00:55:51,014
Shut up!

727
00:55:54,309 --> 00:55:55,560
I have something to tell you.

728
00:55:57,354 --> 00:55:58,188
I said, shut up!

729
00:55:58,271 --> 00:55:59,105
Wait a second!

730
00:55:59,189 --> 00:56:00,440
Hold on.

731
00:56:00,523 --> 00:56:02,108
I'm Ma Mun-mak from Gyodang-ri!

732
00:56:12,452 --> 00:56:13,286
What?

733
00:56:14,120 --> 00:56:17,123
Geum-ok, what did he just say?

734
00:56:17,207 --> 00:56:19,834
Oh my, what are your ears for?

735
00:56:20,543 --> 00:56:22,087
He said we're not getting kicked out.

736
00:56:24,047 --> 00:56:25,507
How's that possible?

737
00:56:27,884 --> 00:56:29,010
Ga-kyung.

738
00:56:29,719 --> 00:56:30,887
Why don't you explain?

739
00:56:31,471 --> 00:56:33,306
The land belongs to us now?

740
00:56:33,390 --> 00:56:34,474
What on earth does it mean?

741
00:56:36,267 --> 00:56:37,602
It's true.

742
00:56:38,686 --> 00:56:41,314
We were about to be thrown out
by Joil Textile Factory,

743
00:56:41,398 --> 00:56:44,109
but Mr. Kim Tae-ung here
took care of everything.

744
00:56:44,192 --> 00:56:46,861
So we can live in peace.
There's nothing to worry about.

745
00:56:46,945 --> 00:56:48,071
Kim Tae-ung?

746
00:56:48,738 --> 00:56:51,616
Aren't you the adopted son of some
big shot at the Government-General?

747
00:56:51,699 --> 00:56:53,201
Why do you use a Joseon name?

748
00:56:55,829 --> 00:56:58,915
Did you change your name
to marry into the Joil Textile family?

749
00:56:59,499 --> 00:57:00,959
That's not it. He's--

750
00:57:03,670 --> 00:57:04,754
I broke off the engagement.

751
00:57:04,838 --> 00:57:06,214
- What?
- Broke off the engagement?

752
00:57:07,549 --> 00:57:09,884
I never wanted to be part of that family.
That's why…

753
00:57:11,261 --> 00:57:12,387
I broke off the engagement.

754
00:57:12,470 --> 00:57:14,806
Don't try to fool us.

755
00:57:14,889 --> 00:57:16,891
You're trying to lower our guard

756
00:57:16,975 --> 00:57:19,811
so you can stab us in the back later.

757
00:57:19,894 --> 00:57:22,105
- Exactly.
- That's not it.

758
00:57:22,188 --> 00:57:23,022
No, listen…

759
00:57:25,900 --> 00:57:27,360
Why won't anyone believe…

760
00:57:37,954 --> 00:57:39,789
Ga-kyung is not lying right now.

761
00:57:41,499 --> 00:57:42,542
I'm certain.

762
00:57:43,418 --> 00:57:44,794
I'd bet my life on it.

763
00:57:45,295 --> 00:57:47,422
So I've decided to

764
00:57:48,715 --> 00:57:50,467
trust Kim Tae-ung.

765
00:57:51,885 --> 00:57:53,970
I'm confused now that No-ma's said that.

766
00:57:54,053 --> 00:57:55,054
That's right.

767
00:57:55,138 --> 00:57:57,682
No-ma wouldn't go that far
if it weren't true.

768
00:57:57,765 --> 00:58:00,226
Yeah, we should trust Ga-kyung.

769
00:58:00,310 --> 00:58:02,395
I think we can trust them.

770
00:58:02,479 --> 00:58:06,608
If it weren't true, would
Joil Textile Factory have left us alone?

771
00:58:06,691 --> 00:58:07,901
- Exactly.
- That's true.

772
00:58:41,434 --> 00:58:42,519
Thank you.

773
00:58:50,693 --> 00:58:52,153
Ga-kyung.

774
00:58:54,989 --> 00:58:55,907
Kkot-nim.

775
00:59:07,794 --> 00:59:08,836
Hit him for me.

776
00:59:09,546 --> 00:59:10,421
Ouch.

777
00:59:20,265 --> 00:59:21,099
Look at that.

778
00:59:23,601 --> 00:59:24,978
What's this thing?

779
00:59:26,229 --> 00:59:27,272
I've never seen it before.

780
00:59:27,355 --> 00:59:28,565
Kkot-nim.

781
00:59:28,648 --> 00:59:30,692
Why don't you sit here?

782
00:59:32,819 --> 00:59:35,071
Ga-kyung, this chair is so soft.

783
00:59:37,657 --> 00:59:38,491
Kkot-nim.

784
00:59:39,867 --> 00:59:43,121
This is called a wheelchair.

785
00:59:46,416 --> 00:59:47,584
Look.

786
00:59:49,377 --> 00:59:50,587
This chair moves on wheels.

787
00:59:50,670 --> 00:59:51,754
Wow.

788
00:59:52,255 --> 00:59:54,424
Now you can go wherever you want.

789
00:59:54,507 --> 00:59:58,219
And my little sister uses one of these.

790
00:59:59,220 --> 01:00:00,471
Let's play together sometime.

791
01:00:00,555 --> 01:00:02,390
Sure, thank you.

792
01:00:02,473 --> 01:00:05,685
No-ma, look, I can make the chair move.

793
01:00:07,520 --> 01:00:08,438
That's really cool.

794
01:00:08,521 --> 01:00:09,689
You sure can.

795
01:00:09,772 --> 01:00:11,441
Good for you, Kkot-nim.

796
01:00:15,445 --> 01:00:16,821
Thank you.

797
01:00:16,904 --> 01:00:17,780
I really mean it.

798
01:00:22,702 --> 01:00:23,870
This is wonderful.

799
01:00:35,214 --> 01:00:36,257
I should get going.

800
01:00:36,883 --> 01:00:38,092
See you tomorrow.

801
01:01:03,868 --> 01:01:05,662
I grew up in a place like this too.

802
01:01:09,582 --> 01:01:12,919
As an orphan, I begged for food.
I nearly starved.

803
01:01:17,048 --> 01:01:18,383
Then people like all of you

804
01:01:19,342 --> 01:01:20,802
took me in and raised me,

805
01:01:20,885 --> 01:01:23,179
even sharing your millet porridge
when food was scarce.

806
01:01:25,306 --> 01:01:26,140
But

807
01:01:27,642 --> 01:01:29,560
I hated that place.

808
01:01:32,105 --> 01:01:32,980
I wanted to…

809
01:01:34,857 --> 01:01:36,693
eat my fill at least once before I died.

810
01:01:39,153 --> 01:01:41,406
So I chose to become
a Japanese man's adopted son.

811
01:01:45,910 --> 01:01:47,078
That's why…

812
01:01:50,748 --> 01:01:52,750
I don't deserve to stand before you.

813
01:01:59,006 --> 01:02:01,634
I wasn't trying to be kind

814
01:02:03,386 --> 01:02:05,346
by giving this land back to you.

815
01:02:17,692 --> 01:02:19,777
I wanted to make up for it,
even just a little.

816
01:02:30,329 --> 01:02:32,039
It's supposed to be a happy day.

817
01:02:32,915 --> 01:02:34,250
Sorry for putting a damper on it.

818
01:02:44,135 --> 01:02:45,887
Teacher.

819
01:03:08,117 --> 01:03:08,951
Thank you.

820
01:03:13,956 --> 01:03:15,041
Thanks.

821
01:03:15,124 --> 01:03:16,209
We're grateful.

822
01:03:16,292 --> 01:03:17,126
Thanks so much.

823
01:03:17,210 --> 01:03:18,711
- Thank you.
- Thanks a lot.

824
01:03:18,795 --> 01:03:20,880
- Thank you.
- Thanks.

825
01:03:20,963 --> 01:03:21,798
Thanks, man.

826
01:03:21,881 --> 01:03:23,549
- Thank you.
- We appreciate it.

827
01:03:23,633 --> 01:03:25,718
- We're truly grateful.
- Thank you.

828
01:03:29,430 --> 01:03:31,891
You came back and remembered us.

829
01:03:34,644 --> 01:03:35,978
That's all that matters.

830
01:03:39,774 --> 01:03:40,858
Thank you.

831
01:03:41,984 --> 01:03:43,402
- Thank you so much.
- Thanks.

832
01:03:43,986 --> 01:03:46,823
- I can't thank you enough.
- Thank you.

833
01:03:46,906 --> 01:03:49,033
Thank you for letting us stay here.

834
01:03:54,497 --> 01:03:56,249
We're so grateful.

835
01:03:56,332 --> 01:03:58,835
- Thank you.
- What a handsome young man.

836
01:03:59,669 --> 01:04:01,295
We're truly grateful.

837
01:04:04,298 --> 01:04:05,800
- Thank you.
- Really, thank you.

838
01:04:08,052 --> 01:04:09,929
What's with the tears?

839
01:04:16,060 --> 01:04:17,937
- Thank you.
- Thanks a lot.

840
01:04:26,112 --> 01:04:30,449
<i>If I had a million won</i>

841
01:04:30,533 --> 01:04:33,619
<i>I'd buy a gold hairpin and a fancy ring</i>

842
01:04:33,703 --> 01:04:34,871
Nice.

843
01:04:34,954 --> 01:04:37,415
<i>And I'd even buy a plane</i>

844
01:04:37,498 --> 01:04:38,708
Have a drink.

845
01:04:38,791 --> 01:04:40,334
- Sure.
- Yeah.

846
01:04:40,418 --> 01:04:41,460
Here.

847
01:04:41,544 --> 01:04:43,004
Give me a drink.

848
01:04:47,842 --> 01:04:48,718
Tae-ung!

849
01:04:49,427 --> 01:04:51,012
I'm sorry about the other day.

850
01:04:54,765 --> 01:04:56,475
<i>And fly high above the clouds</i>

851
01:04:56,559 --> 01:04:58,352
<i>Spinning round and round</i>

852
01:05:03,357 --> 01:05:06,986
<i>No, stop dreaming of a million won</i>

853
01:05:07,778 --> 01:05:11,407
<i>Or you'll waste your youth</i>
<i>Chasing a foolish dream</i>

854
01:05:11,490 --> 01:05:12,325
One more time.

855
01:05:20,458 --> 01:05:21,459
Hold on.

856
01:05:29,175 --> 01:05:30,760
Here, have some water.

857
01:05:32,762 --> 01:05:33,804
Thank you.

858
01:05:40,853 --> 01:05:41,687
Are you okay?

859
01:05:42,355 --> 01:05:43,731
Yes, it's nothing.

860
01:05:44,607 --> 01:05:46,692
I breathed in some smoke from the bonfire.

861
01:05:46,776 --> 01:05:50,488
Maybe it's the smoke, but lately,
you've been pushing yourself too hard.

862
01:05:52,239 --> 01:05:53,616
I'm all right, really.

863
01:06:04,794 --> 01:06:05,836
It's all going to be okay.

864
01:06:08,422 --> 01:06:11,801
We've turned them against each other
and secured Gyodang-ri, just as planned.

865
01:06:12,510 --> 01:06:15,137
We've put the marriage contract
in Fukuda's hands.

866
01:06:15,721 --> 01:06:17,598
We've done all we can on our end.

867
01:06:18,474 --> 01:06:21,268
So now we just have
to wait a little longer.

868
01:06:21,352 --> 01:06:22,728
It's all going to work out,

869
01:06:23,437 --> 01:06:24,313
I promise.

870
01:06:28,609 --> 01:06:31,112
You're looking at me
like you don't believe me.

871
01:06:36,033 --> 01:06:37,785
Here, look.

872
01:06:40,162 --> 01:06:42,331
In the West, crossing your fingers means

873
01:06:42,957 --> 01:06:45,960
you're wishing someone luck.

874
01:06:46,043 --> 01:06:46,919
But

875
01:06:47,628 --> 01:06:50,006
when you cross them
like this while you're lying,

876
01:06:50,089 --> 01:06:54,468
it means you're hoping
your lie won't be found out.

877
01:06:55,511 --> 01:06:58,431
So whenever I tell a lie,
I cross my fingers like this.

878
01:06:58,514 --> 01:07:00,141
I've never been caught.

879
01:07:02,393 --> 01:07:03,436
I'm serious.

880
01:07:03,519 --> 01:07:04,729
Now look for yourself.

881
01:07:04,812 --> 01:07:06,230
I'm not crossing my fingers.

882
01:07:06,814 --> 01:07:09,400
So everything's going to be fine.

883
01:07:12,611 --> 01:07:13,487
I like you.

884
01:07:22,079 --> 01:07:22,913
Hold on.

885
01:07:24,582 --> 01:07:25,958
"I like you."

886
01:07:26,042 --> 01:07:28,169
You told me that
on your first day at work.

887
01:07:29,045 --> 01:07:30,337
So were you lying to me

888
01:07:31,005 --> 01:07:33,174
with your fingers crossed back then?

889
01:07:34,759 --> 01:07:35,593
Well…

890
01:07:36,177 --> 01:07:37,011
I mean…

891
01:07:39,096 --> 01:07:40,431
Back then…

892
01:07:43,225 --> 01:07:45,269
I can't quite remember.

893
01:07:46,729 --> 01:07:49,398
Aren't you lying right now?

894
01:07:49,482 --> 01:07:50,816
No, I'm telling the truth.

895
01:07:50,900 --> 01:07:52,651
Look, I'm not crossing my fingers.

896
01:07:53,486 --> 01:07:55,362
Why won't you believe me?

897
01:07:58,616 --> 01:07:59,617
I told you before.

898
01:08:01,619 --> 01:08:04,705
You're not the only one who can lie.

899
01:08:06,749 --> 01:08:08,209
Are you kidding me?

900
01:08:12,213 --> 01:08:14,173
I shouldn't have taught you that.

901
01:08:22,973 --> 01:08:25,142
Right before graduating
from the Army Academy…

902
01:08:27,144 --> 01:08:28,646
I came down with tuberculosis.

903
01:08:30,773 --> 01:08:33,776
So I never became a soldier.

904
01:08:37,905 --> 01:08:38,864
So that's why.

905
01:08:42,034 --> 01:08:43,786
My father was very disappointed.

906
01:08:44,954 --> 01:08:45,788
But

907
01:08:47,540 --> 01:08:50,668
when I found out I couldn't become
a soldier because of my illness,

908
01:08:51,293 --> 01:08:52,169
I was actually…

909
01:08:54,922 --> 01:08:56,048
relieved.

910
01:08:58,092 --> 01:09:00,010
I only enrolled in the Army Academy

911
01:09:01,679 --> 01:09:03,055
to please my father.

912
01:09:04,140 --> 01:09:07,852
I acted like being a soldier was my dream,
though I never wanted to be one.

913
01:09:12,356 --> 01:09:13,524
But now…

914
01:09:15,734 --> 01:09:17,069
I'm trying to…

915
01:09:19,989 --> 01:09:21,866
bring my father down.

916
01:09:23,450 --> 01:09:25,035
I feel so…

917
01:09:33,878 --> 01:09:34,962
strange.

918
01:09:51,312 --> 01:09:52,771
Listen.

919
01:09:54,648 --> 01:09:55,482
Yes?

920
01:10:00,571 --> 01:10:03,449
<i>If you found out your mother was a spy</i>

921
01:10:04,617 --> 01:10:08,245
<i>and your father was</i>
<i>the one who killed her…</i>

922
01:10:10,497 --> 01:10:14,960
<i>could you handle the truth?</i>

923
01:10:20,257 --> 01:10:21,383
What was

924
01:10:22,468 --> 01:10:24,386
your mother like?

925
01:10:42,196 --> 01:10:43,197
My mother…

926
01:10:49,286 --> 01:10:50,746
didn't love me.

927
01:10:54,833 --> 01:10:55,876
She left me

928
01:10:57,419 --> 01:10:58,587
behind.

929
01:11:04,677 --> 01:11:05,511
It had been…

930
01:11:07,763 --> 01:11:08,931
lodged like a thorn

931
01:11:09,974 --> 01:11:11,725
deep down in my heart…

932
01:11:14,770 --> 01:11:16,522
for a very long time.

933
01:11:23,362 --> 01:11:25,114
But I'm all right now.

934
01:11:33,998 --> 01:11:35,416
Because I met you.

935
01:11:41,880 --> 01:11:43,299
Once I find my mother,

936
01:11:45,134 --> 01:11:46,302
I'll tell her…

937
01:11:49,096 --> 01:11:50,222
that her son

938
01:11:51,473 --> 01:11:52,349
met…

939
01:12:00,941 --> 01:12:03,610
someone who is very…

940
01:12:04,862 --> 01:12:05,946
very extraordinary.

941
01:12:32,389 --> 01:12:34,141
Come on. You're lying.

942
01:12:34,224 --> 01:12:37,519
- Which one of you can run the fastest?
- No way.

943
01:12:37,603 --> 01:12:38,520
- Me!
- Me!

944
01:12:39,188 --> 01:12:41,565
You kids couldn't beat me
even if you teamed up.

945
01:12:42,858 --> 01:12:45,152
Back when I was fighting for independence,

946
01:12:45,235 --> 01:12:47,780
- I ran so fast
- Did Tae-ung leave?

947
01:12:47,863 --> 01:12:49,239
you couldn't see my feet.

948
01:12:49,323 --> 01:12:50,866
I told him to get some sleep.

949
01:12:50,949 --> 01:12:53,702
- I was so fast…
- He's been dealing with a lot lately.

950
01:12:54,787 --> 01:12:55,871
Kkot-nim is asleep too.

951
01:12:55,954 --> 01:12:57,039
I see.

952
01:12:58,082 --> 01:13:02,795
Do you think it's okay
if I call Tae-ung "brother"?

953
01:13:03,754 --> 01:13:04,671
I don't see why not.

954
01:13:05,964 --> 01:13:07,591
I've got a big brother now.

955
01:13:07,674 --> 01:13:10,260
Speaking of my days
in the independence movement,

956
01:13:10,344 --> 01:13:11,595
let me tell you a story.

957
01:13:11,678 --> 01:13:14,723
Listen to Bak-chun's story
about the independence movement.

958
01:13:14,807 --> 01:13:16,266
It's really good.

959
01:13:16,350 --> 01:13:17,226
It was

960
01:13:18,018 --> 01:13:20,104
on a winter night in 1925.

961
01:13:20,187 --> 01:13:22,773
With a hundred manifestos
tucked inside my clothes,

962
01:13:23,816 --> 01:13:26,819
I was making my way
through the pitch-black night.

963
01:13:28,779 --> 01:13:30,072
That scared me.

964
01:13:30,155 --> 01:13:32,157
This whistle nearly split my eardrums.

965
01:13:32,741 --> 01:13:33,575
So I scoffed

966
01:13:34,243 --> 01:13:36,203
and thought, "Ha, no big deal,"

967
01:13:36,286 --> 01:13:38,539
then got ready to make a run for it.

968
01:13:39,748 --> 01:13:40,833
Wouldn't you know it?

969
01:13:41,375 --> 01:13:42,584
I got a cramp in my leg.

970
01:13:42,668 --> 01:13:44,086
- Oh no.
- Oh dear.

971
01:13:44,169 --> 01:13:47,381
I thought,
"This is it. Today is the day I die."

972
01:13:48,257 --> 01:13:49,591
Just then, there it was.

973
01:13:50,884 --> 01:13:52,469
I saw Jongno Pawnshop up ahead.

974
01:13:53,429 --> 01:13:55,931
"Thank goodness!" I thought

975
01:13:56,014 --> 01:14:00,018
as I dragged myself over there
and dropped the bundle of manifestos!

976
01:14:00,102 --> 01:14:00,936
Mr. Lee!

977
01:14:02,479 --> 01:14:03,522
Hold on.

978
01:14:04,148 --> 01:14:05,774
What was that you just said?

979
01:14:06,400 --> 01:14:07,443
Come on, Ga-kyung.

980
01:14:08,318 --> 01:14:10,070
I was just getting to the best part.

981
01:14:10,154 --> 01:14:11,488
No, you just said

982
01:14:12,072 --> 01:14:14,032
"Jongno Pawnshop," didn't you?

983
01:14:14,116 --> 01:14:15,409
Yes, Jongno Pawnshop.

984
01:14:16,034 --> 01:14:17,202
Where exactly is it?

985
01:14:17,286 --> 01:14:20,372
I've searched all over Jongno
and never found it.

986
01:14:23,959 --> 01:14:25,127
Of course you couldn't.

987
01:14:25,961 --> 01:14:27,171
What?

988
01:14:27,254 --> 01:14:30,799
Jongno Pawnshop
was never really a pawnshop.

989
01:14:35,929 --> 01:14:37,306
<i>Hanseong Medicine Clinic.</i>

990
01:14:39,766 --> 01:14:43,061
<i>Hanseong Medicine Clinic</i>
<i>in Jongno was actually</i>

991
01:14:44,021 --> 01:14:45,022
<i>Jongno Pawnshop.</i>

992
01:14:50,277 --> 01:14:53,071
<i>Back in those days,</i>
<i>members of the independence movement</i>

993
01:14:53,155 --> 01:14:56,783
<i>would secretly use the clinic</i>
<i>to pass messages…</i>

994
01:14:59,286 --> 01:15:00,871
Please wait here for a while.

995
01:15:07,711 --> 01:15:10,839
<i>…or hide things</i>
<i>they couldn't let anyone discover.</i>

996
01:15:12,633 --> 01:15:14,176
<i>So we secretly called it</i>

997
01:15:14,885 --> 01:15:17,554
<i>"Jongno Pawnshop." It was our code name.</i>

998
01:15:17,638 --> 01:15:18,722
HWANG MUN-O

999
01:15:18,805 --> 01:15:21,892
<i>That's why you'll never find</i>
<i>Jongno Pawnshop in Jongno.</i>

1000
01:15:21,975 --> 01:15:25,646
<i>To show their respect</i>
<i>for the independence movement,</i>

1001
01:15:25,729 --> 01:15:29,066
<i>no Joseon person in Jongno</i>
<i>would use that name for their business.</i>

1002
01:15:29,149 --> 01:15:30,192
How about that?

1003
01:15:30,901 --> 01:15:31,777
So?

1004
01:15:31,860 --> 01:15:33,195
Quite an amazing story, isn't it?

1005
01:15:33,278 --> 01:15:34,905
- Yes.
- Yes!

1006
01:15:34,988 --> 01:15:35,906
Where's the applause?

1007
01:15:39,618 --> 01:15:41,078
Jongno Pawnshop

1008
01:15:42,079 --> 01:15:43,789
was Hanseong Medicine Clinic?

1009
01:15:59,179 --> 01:16:00,097
Who is it?

1010
01:16:03,433 --> 01:16:04,309
What brings you here?

1011
01:16:04,393 --> 01:16:06,144
Why are you here alone?

1012
01:16:09,398 --> 01:16:10,566
I came to pick this up.

1013
01:16:13,860 --> 01:16:15,112
A pawn ticket?

1014
01:16:15,195 --> 01:16:16,488
SIN TAE-JIN

1015
01:16:16,572 --> 01:16:18,115
Sin Tae-jin.

1016
01:16:20,200 --> 01:16:22,369
How did this end up in your hands?

1017
01:16:23,453 --> 01:16:24,538
I'd like to claim it.

1018
01:16:26,039 --> 01:16:29,585
It's been a long time
since we used this place to pass messages.

1019
01:16:30,711 --> 01:16:33,672
But this one, under the name Sin Tae-jin,
went unclaimed for over a decade.

1020
01:16:34,423 --> 01:16:36,174
We thought something might have happened

1021
01:16:36,258 --> 01:16:38,510
or that they might be in jail,
so we held on to it.

1022
01:16:40,512 --> 01:16:42,347
I never expected you to come for it.

1023
01:16:44,141 --> 01:16:46,393
<i>Mokdanpi </i>and <i>sansuyu.</i>

1024
01:16:46,893 --> 01:16:47,728
Here they are.

1025
01:16:49,104 --> 01:16:49,938
<i>Sesin.</i>

1026
01:16:53,775 --> 01:16:54,693
<i>Seontae.</i>

1027
01:16:55,902 --> 01:16:56,945
<i>Jinpi.</i>

1028
01:16:58,155 --> 01:16:58,989
<i>Sintaejin.</i>

1029
01:17:00,824 --> 01:17:03,118
"<i>Sintaejin</i>" is not a person's name.

1030
01:17:06,371 --> 01:17:08,874
It's a code telling you
where the secret drawer is.

1031
01:17:17,924 --> 01:17:21,219
I don't know who left it here,
but I must keep my promise to them.

1032
01:17:23,764 --> 01:17:24,598
Here you go.

1033
01:17:39,446 --> 01:17:40,280
What is this?

1034
01:17:44,034 --> 01:17:45,077
Your mother

1035
01:17:46,078 --> 01:17:49,581
left this at Jongno Pawnshop.

1036
01:17:50,666 --> 01:17:51,792
What?

1037
01:17:52,918 --> 01:17:54,753
I found Jongno Pawnshop.

1038
01:17:57,172 --> 01:18:00,425
I got this from there just now.

1039
01:18:41,341 --> 01:18:44,052
Why are these two…

1040
01:19:09,369 --> 01:19:10,203
Mother.

1041
01:19:10,287 --> 01:19:11,413
Mom.

1042
01:19:24,384 --> 01:19:25,469
Your mom?

1043
01:19:44,404 --> 01:19:45,238
Well…

1044
01:19:45,864 --> 01:19:46,990
Hold on.

1045
01:21:00,438 --> 01:21:04,359
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

1046
01:21:04,442 --> 01:21:05,944
<i>I am</i>

1047
01:21:06,027 --> 01:21:07,737
her daughter.

1048
01:21:08,947 --> 01:21:11,157
<i>How are you any different?</i>

1049
01:21:11,241 --> 01:21:12,701
<i>Isn't this a threat too?</i>

1050
01:21:13,535 --> 01:21:14,536
Kim Tae-ung.

1051
01:21:15,245 --> 01:21:17,622
<i>I owe your mother a debt.</i>

1052
01:21:17,706 --> 01:21:20,041
<i>This tip finally gave us a lead.</i>

1053
01:21:20,125 --> 01:21:21,751
<i>Everybody, run!</i>

1054
01:21:25,213 --> 01:21:26,339
<i>- Mr. Sato!</i>
<i>- Ga-kyung.</i>

1055
01:21:26,965 --> 01:21:28,466
<i>Mr. Sato!</i>

1056
01:21:29,092 --> 01:21:31,386
<i>Just died for nothing</i>

1057
01:21:31,469 --> 01:21:33,763
<i>because of you!</i>

1058
01:21:40,645 --> 01:21:42,647
Subtitle translation by: Youji Kim
